<Project>

  <!--
    SQLitePCLRaw.lib.e_sqlite3 2.1.12 breaks every .NET Framework build with MSB3030.

    Its buildTransitive/net461/SQLitePCLRaw.lib.e_sqlite3.targets unconditionally declares

        <Content Include="...\runtimes\win-arm\native\e_sqlite3.dll">

    but 2.1.12 does not ship a runtimes/win-arm folder. The package now contains only
    win-arm64, win-x64 and win-x86; the ARM32 runtime was dropped and the Content item that
    copies it was left behind. 2.1.6 and 2.1.11 both shipped runtimes/win-arm, so this is a
    regression introduced in 2.1.12 rather than long-standing behaviour.

    Only the net461 targets carry these items, so only .NET Framework target frameworks are
    affected — net471 here. net6.0 and later declare no such items and build fine, which is
    why the failure looks intermittent: it disappears the moment you build a single modern
    target framework with -f.

    The result is:

        error MSB3030: Could not copy the file
        "...\sqlitepclraw.lib.e_sqlite3\2.1.12\runtimes\win-arm\native\e_sqlite3.dll"
        because it was not found.

    Pinning the package down to 2.1.11 is not an option: Microsoft.Data.Sqlite 10.0.11
    requires 2.1.12, so an explicit lower pin is a NU1605 downgrade. Instead, drop any
    Content item this package contributes that does not exist on disk. That is narrow (it
    cannot hide a missing file from any other package), self-correcting (it does nothing
    once a future release stops declaring the item, or starts shipping it again), and needs
    no dependency surgery.
  -->
  <Target Name="RemoveMissingSqlitePclRawNativeContent" BeforeTargets="AssignTargetPaths">
    <ItemGroup>
      <Content Remove="@(Content)"
               Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('%(Content.Identity)').ToLowerInvariant().Contains('sqlitepclraw')) AND !Exists('%(Content.Identity)')" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

</Project>
